Z||000002||Shiner||05-04-2000||11:42 PM||||V8Archie is the one who says stuff like "we have a car with a supercharged 426 Hemi running nitromethane hooked up to a Isuzu, and have had no problems for 200,000 miles"  Personally, I think he is full of **** .||||reg||1
Z||000003||TooLowFiero||05-05-2000||12:52 AM||||GT,<BR>if you werent in SD since im in Wi i have a 4 sp you could buy for like 75 bucks its out of a 84 SE sorry i cant help ya<P>Bobby||||reg||1
Z||000004||Terrybogin||05-05-2000||01:04 AM||||It was my understanding that the shift forks were the weak link in the Isuzu tranny. That speed shifting or jerking the shifter around would shorten the life of the forks. Hopefully someone here will know because I will have an Isuzu hooked to my 3.4 sometime in the near future.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||05-05-2000||01:32 AM||||Archie only installs chevy 350's not 426 hemi's. Anyway there are a few people in our club that have V-8's in their cars and some have the Izusu 5spd. I don't know what the "abuse" factor is with the forks but the Izusu 5spd works fine with a 300hp V-8. I have seen other websites that say different but I think it's just to get you to purchase a more expensive trans. IMHO||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||05-05-2000||01:36 AM||||Yeah, the isuzu has weak shift forks that are prone to breakage if you speed shift or jam the gears.  <B>Any</B> tranny will last forever if you take care of it...  The 84 4-spd muncie has a weaker tranny case than the 85-86 4-spd's, and I think we all know about the weak synchros on the getrag  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> grrrr..<P>Although I did find a temporary fix for my 3rd gear grind... double clutch!  doesn't grind one bit then...||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||05-05-2000||02:48 AM||||Well, it doesn't sound too bad.  I'm not the type of person to abuse my car, although I occasionally like to flog her when she's been naughty.  bad88 suggested we tear it down in my garage and swap the tranny in.  I was really hesitant in doing this by myself, but I think we can easily pull it off with his experience.  Looks like I'll prolly go Isuzu.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||TheFiero64||05-05-2000||08:25 AM||||I've been at least 12k with my isuzu mounted to my 2.8 V6 and have had no prob. I love the gearing and power I get on acceleration. I have never driven the getrag though. I have driven the 4 speed and don't like it.  The tranny has 160K on it and shifts great. So I say use it.||||reg||10
Z||000009||ChadMan||05-05-2000||08:49 AM||||I posted a thread a while back asking for anyone who had first-hand experience with an Isuzu failing to post it, and no one did - the only responses were people who were perfectly happy with their Isuzu.<P>Go Isuzu - mine has 109K on it and shifts great.  They're cheap, too.||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||05-05-2000||12:31 PM||||Hi ChadMan,<P>I've been brutal to the Isuzu in my X-11 (no, it's not putting out 200HP even with roller everything and a cam that makes you seasick at the light). Still working after 40k.<P>Does anyone have 100k miles on any V8 swap?<P>Most of these trans will handle it for awhile. The beauty of the Isuzu is that they are a dime-a-dozen so even if they die in 25k, you just grab another one and keep going. I agree that if you avoid tons of speed shifting, the Isuzu will hold up for a long time.<P>Psycho Beaver<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-05-2000).]||||reg||14
